rved_research_question: Play-type labeling audit: OTHER/DRIVE foul leakage\n- auto_outcome_status: artifact\n- observed_hypothesis: Play-type PPP differences are real efficiency, not labeling/foul artifacts.\n- observed_result: OTHER: 36% end in fouls, PPP inflated (FOUL_BUCKET). DRIVE: 12pp below foul benchmark (FOUL_LEAK). PNR shows 22% foul rate. Only 335 of 50,668 possessions (0.7%) map to a real play type via initiation_type.\n- auto_failure_mechanism: C_artifact (apparent effect produced by a construction, selection or labelling flaw)\n- observed_failure_evidence: OTHER PPP inflated by 36% foul endings; DRIVE 12pp below foul benchmark; initiation_type maps 0.7%.\n- observed_sample_and_power: 50,668 possessions in the initiation_type check\n- auto_validation_status: no_validation_recorded; evidence: NOT_RECORDED\n- observed_method: Per-PT diagnostic of foul-ending share vs PPP; initiation_type mapping.\n- observed_verdict: artifact_found"}], "n_results": 2}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
 "research_concepts": [
  "Play-type analysis",
  "Defensive transition",
  "Sequential effects in basketball",
  "Performance-based play labeling",
  "Points Per Possession (PPP)"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "Causal inference in sequential sports data",
  "Controlling for confounding variables in play-by-play analysis",
  "Defining possession boundaries for PPP calculation",
  "Modeling lag effects",
  "Generating or validating 'AVOID' labels"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF07",
   "status_as_recorded": "supported",
   "finding": "After the opponent runs play type X, the next offensive play type Y scores above Y's unconditional H2 baseline, and every PT has a defensive aftershock. A2 aftershock 8/8 PTs replicate all-games and playoffs (CUT +0.119 n=21,398 / PO +0.131 n=1,145 through TRANSITION +0.135 n=26,262 / +0.126 n=1,529). All-games aftershock after success: CUT +0.119 (n=21398), DRIVE +0.123 (57621), PNR +0.127 (51194), POST_UP +0.128 (20619), PULL_UP +0.132 (26060), PUTBACK +0.130 (5888), SPOT_UP +0.128 (43857), TRANSITION +0."
  },
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-025",
   "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
   "finding": "Play-type PPP differences are real efficiency, not labeling/foul artifacts. OTHER: 36% end in fouls, PPP inflated (FOUL_BUCKET). DRIVE: 12pp below foul benchmark (FOUL_LEAK). PNR shows 22% foul rate. Only 335 of 50,668 possessions (0.7%) map to a real play type via initiation_type."
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-025",
   "what_failed": "Assuming play-type PPP differences are real efficiency and not labeling/foul artifacts.",
   "why": "C_artifact (apparent effect produced by a construction, selection or labelling flaw)"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "The existence of a 'defensive aftershock' where, after a team runs a specific play type, the opponent's next possession results in a higher PPP than their baseline, for all 8 observed play types (FAM:SF07).",
  "That sequential effects or 'lag effects' between possessions are measurable and exist.",
  "That play-type PPP calculations, particularly for 'OTHER' and 'DRIVE' play types, can be significantly influenced by labeling artifacts such as foul leakage, leading to inflated or understated PPP values (FAM:SF-CHT-025)."
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "Whether the magnitude of the observed defensive aftershock is 'large enough to drive AVOID labels,' as the definition and threshold for 'AVOID labels' are not provided.",
  "The specific causal mechanisms or player/team-level factors contributing to the defensive aftershock.",
  "The extent to which foul leakage and labeling issues affect other play types beyond 'OTHER', 'DRIVE', and 'PNR', or how these issues specifically bias the 'defensive aftershock' metric.",
  "How 'own-sequence lags' should be defined, measured, or integrated into an 'AVOID' label decision, beyond the general concept of sequential pair premiums."
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "The research question's premise that 'opponent next-possession PPP after you run play X is large enough to drive AVOID labels' is supported in principle by the documented 'defensive aftershock', which shows a measurable increase in opponent PPP.",
  "The definition and methodology for creating 'AVOID' labels will be critical and must account for potential artifacts in PPP measurement.",
  "Controlling for confounders such as score differential, game state, opponent strength, and player personnel will be crucial to isolate the effect of the preceding play type."
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "The precise definition of 'AVOID labels' and the threshold for 'large enough' are not specified.",
  "The specific 'own-sequence lags' to be investigated are not detailed (e.g., how many possessions back, which outcomes matter).",
  "The full extent of play-type labeling accuracy issues for all play types and their impact on sequential PPP measurements remains unclear."
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes",
  "explanation": "The historical evidence confirmed the existence of measurable sequential effects (defensive aftershock and sequential pair premiums), which makes the core premise of the research question testable. However, it also introduced a critical methodological risk (FAM:SF-CHT-025) regarding play-type labeling artifacts, particularly for 'OTHER' and 'DRIVE' PPP. This necessitates a change in the research design to explicitly audit and potentially adjust PPP calculations for known labeling biases, and to define 'AVOID' labels with caution."
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": 0.8,
  "blocking_constraints": []
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "partly",
  "cited": [
   "FAM:SF07"
  ],
  "summary": "FAM:SF07 established that a 'defensive aftershock' exists for all 8 major play types, where the opponent's next possession yields a higher PPP. This directly supports the idea that 'opponent next-possession PPP after you run play X is large enough' to be a measurable phenomenon. It also noted the existence of sequential pair premiums, indicating that own-sequence lags matter. However, it did not define 'AVOID' labels or assess whether the magnitude of the aftershock is sufficient for such a purpose."
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "LABEL_ERROR",
   "text": "Play-type labels, especially for 'OTHER' and 'DRIVE', are known to have foul leakage issues that inflate or deflate PPP. This could lead to spurious 'AVOID' labels if not accounted for. The 'AVOID' label itself, if derived from potentially biased PPP, could be an OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL risk."
  },
  {
   "code": "CONFOUNDING",
   "text": "The observed 'defensive aftershock' or 'own-sequence lags' might be confounded by game state (score differential, time on clock), player personnel on court, opponent strength, or specific tactical decisions not captured by play-type labels. Without proper controls, the causal link between 'play X' and subsequent PPP is unclear."
  },
  {
   "code": "MECHANICAL_FLOOR",
   "text": "PPP has a mechanical floor of 0. If a play sequence frequently results in 0 points due to turnovers or missed shots, differences in PPP might be compressed near this floor, potentially masking true effects or making small differences less meaningful."
  },
  {
   "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
   "text": "While overall aftershock effects are well-powered, investigating specific play-type sequences, especially rare ones or at the team/player level, could lead to small sample sizes and insufficient power, as noted in FAM:SF07 for team-level cells."
  },
  {
   "code": "OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL",
   "text": "The 'AVOID' label is a derived concept based on PPP. The methodology for its derivation (thresholds, aggregation, control for confounders) must be robust to avoid circular reasoning or labeling noise as signal."
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": 0.6,
  "rejected": 0.1,
  "untestable": 0.05,
  "artifact": 0.15,
  "uncertain": 0.1
 },
 "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "The causal effect of running specific offensive play type X (by a team) on the opponent's subsequent possession PPP, and on the team's own subsequent possessions, after controlling for confounding factors. This effect will be used to inform 'AVOID' labels.",
  "unit": "Basketball possession. The primary unit of analysis for the 'aftershock' will be the opponent's possession immediately following a team's possession ending in play type X. For 'own-sequence lags', it will be a team's possession following a prior own-team possession ending in play type Y.",
  "sample": "All NBA regular season and playoff possessions from 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25, and complete 2025-26 seasons (regular season and playoffs through 2026-06-21).",
  "power": "Sufficient power is expected for aggregate play-type effects based on FAM:SF07. Power for specific team/player-level effects or rare play sequences will be assessed during exploratory analysis; a minimum sample size of 500 instances per play type-lag combination will be required for robust inference.",
  "controls": "Statistical regression models (e.g., mixed-effects models) will be used to control for known confounders, including: game state (score differential, quarter, time remaining), player personnel on court (using lineup data), opponent strength (team offensive/defensive ratings), and a baseline PPP for the next possession type. PPP calculations for 'OTHER' and 'DRIVE' play types will be adjusted or flagged based on the foul leakage findings from FAM:SF-CHT-025, possibly by excluding foul-ending possessions or by including foul rate as a covariate.",
  "falsification": "The hypothesis would be falsified if, after controlling for confounders and accounting for labeling artifacts, the next-possession PPP (opponent or own) does not significantly deviate from its expected baseline, or if the magnitude of deviation is consistently below a pre-defined threshold for 'AVOID' labeling (e.g., 0.05 PPP increase).",
  "multiplicity": "Adjustments for multiple comparisons will be applied if testing individual effects for all 8+ play types or multiple lag sequences. A False Discovery Rate (FDR) control method (e.g., Benjamini-Hochberg) will be used.",
  "decision_rule": "The research question will be considered 'supported' if statistically significant and practically meaningful (e.g., >0.05 PPP deviation) 'aftershock' or 'lag' effects are consistently observed across play types, and if these effects are robust to controls and labeling adjustments, thereby providing a basis for 'AVOID' labels. The definition of 'AVOID' labels will be established as a play type where the mean next-possession PPP for the opponent is X standard deviations above the average next-possession PPP, or where own-sequence lags show a significant negative effect on subsequent own-PPP.",
  "data_version": "NBA 2022-23 to 2025-26 (complete as of 2026-06-21)."
 }
}

